{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>This is the book, etc.</b> One needs to know the intent of His saying ״זֶה סֵפֶר״ — what is this account? Further, He said <b>on the day of creating,</b> etc., <b>in the likeness</b> — which appears to mean that on the day of creating he was in the likeness, but not afterward. Further, after He had already made this known on the day of creation, why did He repeat the matter twice? Further, why was it necessary to say twice again ״זָכָר וּנְקֵבָה״ etc., and further why did He return to say twice ״בְּיוֹם הִבָּרְאָם״?" "Now the intent of the verses is to guard the way of HaShem, for all His paths are life eternal. And behold, when Elohim came to arrange the two worlds that were ordered in the generations for several reasons, both revealed and hidden, and it was necessary to mention the death of man and those who issued from his loins, He prefaced by removing the claim of a claimant against Him, blessed be He: How could He reject the labor of His hands? As it were — is there a craftsman who breaks his tools? Is there a builder who demolishes his buildings? Is there one who toils and wastes his toil? And the wonder grows greater when one considers the worth of that which exists and perishes — why would He do so? He is the Craftsman, He is the Creator, He is the Builder of His world for the sake of man, and He breaks him in an instant, so that the delight of all creation is ruined, and the loss touches none but its Master. Therefore, before the verse states the death of man, it preceded and said that the matter did not come from HaShem — to put to death the most cherished generation of creation — but rather he himself brought death upon himself. And that is what it says: <b>This is the book of the generations</b> — meaning, that which man begot through the instrumentality of his deeds, for תּוֹלְדוֹת may be said of children and of deeds, in the manner they said of blessed memory (Tanhuma, Noah) on the passage ״אֵלֶּה תּוֹלְדוֹת נֹחַ״, and they interpreted: the principal generations of the righteous are mitzvot and good deeds. See then — they apply the language of תּוֹלְדוֹת to what a man produces through his conduct; so too here, it declares the תּוֹלְדוֹת of man, for from him issued things contrary to the will of HaShem, for HaShem desired, on the day He created them, that they be בִּדְמוּת אֱלֹהִים. And that is what it says: <b>On the day Elohim created man, in the likeness of Elohim He made him</b> — meaning, He ordained him to live forever, in the manner of what it says (Psalms 82:6): ״אֲנִי אָמַרְתִּי אֱלֹהִים אַתֶּם״ etc., ״אָכֵן כְּאָדָם תְּמוּתוּן״ — this declares that when He said אֱלֹהִים, it meant life eternal. And His saying <b>זָכָר וּנְקֵבָה בְּרָאָם</b> declares that what He said — ״בִּדְמוּת אֱלֹהִים״ etc. — applied also to the female. It further intends to say that although He created them בִּדְמוּת אֱלֹהִים to live forever, He nevertheless created them זָכָר וּנְקֵבָה to be fruitful and multiply." "And He said further, <b>And He blessed</b> etc., <b>and He called their name Adam</b> — which is the most exalted name, for HaShem magnified His kindness toward them in every aspect of excellence. And He concluded by saying <b>on the day they were created</b> — that all the excellences were present on the day of creation, but the matter did not endure, neither in the aspect of the man nor in the aspect of the woman, as is evident from what occurred. And after He made known what came from Him, blessed be He, the verse writes ״וַיְחִי אָדָם״ etc., ״וַיָּמֹת״ — and the matter is self-evident: this did not come from HaShem, nor did He mutilate the work of His hands, but rather man brought it upon himself."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:39:30.677677197Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}